Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-05-31
Subject: Make compiler units use compiler-side align helper
License: expat

Several compiler units call bare integer `align(...)`; the compiler-side
helper is declared in `cutils.pas`.  Import `cutils` explicitly at those
call sites.

In machoutils.AlignAddr additionally wrap the second argument in
`qword(...)`.  cutils.align is overloaded on (longint,longint),
(int64,int64) and (qword,qword); calling it with (PtrUInt, integer)
without the wrap makes the overload resolver rank candidates, and FPC
and tp2cc have different ranking models that do not necessarily agree
on the winner.

FPC (compiler/htypechk.pas:is_better_candidate) assigns each parameter
one of the conversion levels te_exact, te_equal, te_convert_l1..l6,
te_convert_operator, te_incompatible (defined in compiler/symconst.pas,
assigned in compiler/defcmp.pas), then compares two candidates by
per-level parameter counts in priority order, with exact_count
preferring more and ordinal_distance preferring less.

tp2cc (src/emit_resolution_types.h:ConvRank) scores each parameter with
a (rank, distance) pair on its own 14-level ladder, then compares
candidates per parameter.

Wrapping md[...] in qword(...) makes both arguments exact-fit for
align(qword,qword), so neither ranking model is exercised.

--- a/compiler/machoutils.pas
+++ b/compiler/machoutils.pas
@@ -30,6 +30,7 @@
 interface

 uses
+  cutils,
   macho;

   type
@@ -502,7 +503,7 @@
       p   : PtrUInt;
     begin
       p:=addr;
-      p:=align(p, md[cputarget and CPU_ARCH_ABI64 > 0]);
+      p:=align(p, qword(md[cputarget and CPU_ARCH_ABI64 > 0]));
       Result:=qword(p);
     end;

